WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
A metal wire looks perfectly still.

2
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.010
Inside, it is not.

3
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.680
Free electrons drift through
a lattice of positive ions,

4
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.550
colliding again and again
with the vibrating ions.

5
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.180
Each collision passes energy to the ions,

6
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.920
and the metal heats up.

7
00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.550
That is where resistance comes from.

8
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.230
Now grip the wire at both ends and
pull, until it is twice as long.

9
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.340
What happens to its resistance?

10
00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.760
In this mission, you'll calculate the
resistance of a real constantan wire from

11
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.660
its length and diameter.

12
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.980
Then you'll see why a hot metal has
more resistance than a cold one.

13
00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:41.360
Your mission: predict what happens to
the resistance when you pull the wire out

14
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.760
to double its length.
